  "summary": "Recent preprints demonstrate phase-controlled spin waves, wireless magnon-polariton readout, and acoustic and nanomechanical nonlinearity control.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is control of confined spin, photonic, acoustic and mechanical waves through transducers, coupling geometry and nonlinearity. Two preprints use transducers and coupling to set wave behaviour: a VCMA open-resonator transducer provides two controlled phase states for parametric spin-wave excitation with a lowered threshold, and two wirelessly coupled ring resonators enable remote excitation and readout of cavity magnon-polaritons through photonic modes delocalised across a gap. The acoustic study, a 3D-printed labyrinthine meta-atom in a waveguide, relies on symmetry breaking in its bound states in the continuum, while the buckled nanotube resonator shows strong intrinsic nonlinearity, with bistability bounded at 34 phonons and a suggested approach to the single-phonon Kerr regime. The items differ in platform and maturity and do not all share one mechanism. What is new is the emphasis on phase-selective and remote addressing of spin and photonic modes, together with nonlinear mechanical resonators approaching few-phonon operation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
